{
    "map": {
        "category_for_comparison.descr": "<xdoc><p>The neighboring category for the analysis. The tool will identify the degree to which the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Category of Interest</span> is attracted to or isolated from the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Neighboring Category</span>.</p></xdoc>",
        "category_for_comparison.title": "Neighboring Category",
        "category_of_interest.descr": "<xdoc><p>The base category for the analysis. The tool will identify, for each <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Category of Interest</span> value, the degree to which the base category is attracted to or colocated with the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Neighboring Category</span>.</p></xdoc>",
        "category_of_interest.title": "Category of Interest",
        "description": "Measures local patterns of spatial association, or colocation, between two categories of point features using the colocation quotient statistic.",
        "distance_band.descr": "The neighborhood size is a constant or fixed distance for each feature. All features within this distance will be used to test for local relationships between categories. If no value is provided, the distance used will be the average distance at which each feature has at least eight neighbors.",
        "distance_band.title": "Distance Band",
        "field_for_comparison.descr": "<xdoc><p>The field from the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Input Neighboring Features</span> parameter containing the category to be compared.</p></xdoc>",
        "field_for_comparison.title": "Field Containing Neighboring Category",
        "field_of_interest.descr": "The field containing the category or categories to be analyzed.",
        "field_of_interest.title": "Field of Interest",
        "in_features_of_interest.descr": "The feature class containing points with representative categories.",
        "in_features_of_interest.title": "Input Features of Interest",
        "input_feature_for_comparison.descr": "The input feature class containing the points with the categories that will be compared.",
        "input_feature_for_comparison.title": "Input Neighboring Features",
        "input_type.descr": "<xdoc><p>Specifies whether the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Input Features of Interest</span> will come from the same dataset with specified categories, different datasets with specified categories, or different datasets that will be treated as their own category (for example, one dataset with all points representing cheetahs and a second dataset in which all points represent gazelles).</p><ul><li><span title=\"SINGLE_DATASET\">Single dataset</span>—The categories to be analyzed exist in a field in a single dataset.</li><li><span title=\"TWO_DATASETS\">Two datasets</span>—The categories to be analyzed exist in fields of separate datasets.</li><li><span title=\"DATASETS_WITHOUT_CATEGORIES\">Datasets without categories</span>—Two separate datasets representing two categories will be analyzed.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "input_type.domain.DATASETS_WITHOUT_CATEGORIES": "Datasets without categories",
        "input_type.domain.SINGLE_DATASET": "Single dataset",
        "input_type.domain.TWO_DATASETS": "Two datasets",
        "input_type.title": "Input Type",
        "local_weighting_scheme.descr": "<xdoc><p>Specifies the kernel type that will be used to provide the spatial weighting. The kernel defines how each feature is related to other features within its neighborhood.</p><ul><li><span title=\"BISQUARE\">Bisquare</span>—Features will be weighted based on the distance to the farthest neighbor or the edge of the distance band, and a weight of 0 will be assigned to any feature outside the neighborhood specified.</li><li><span title=\"GAUSSIAN\">Gaussian</span>—Features will be weighted based on the distance to the farthest neighbor or the edge of the distance band but drop off more quickly than the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Bisquare</span> option. A weight of 0 will be assigned to any feature outside the neighborhood specified. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"NONE\">None</span>—No weighting scheme will be applied, and all features within the neighborhood will be given a weight of 1 and contribute equally. All features outside the neighborhood will be given a weight of 0.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "local_weighting_scheme.domain.BISQUARE": "Bisquare",
        "local_weighting_scheme.domain.GAUSSIAN": "Gaussian",
        "local_weighting_scheme.domain.NONE": "None",
        "local_weighting_scheme.title": "Local Weighting Scheme",
        "neighborhood_type.descr": "<xdoc><p>Specifies how the spatial relationships among features are defined.</p><ul><li><span title=\"DISTANCE_BAND\">Distance band</span>—Each feature will be analyzed within the context of neighboring features. Neighboring features inside the specified critical distance specified by the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Distance Band</span> parameter receive a weight of one and exert influence on computations for the target feature. Neighboring features outside the critical distance receive a weight of zero and have no influence on a target feature&apos;s computations.</li><li><span title=\"K_NEAREST_NEIGHBORS\">K nearest neighbors</span>—The closest k features will be included in the analysis as neighbors. The number of neighbors is specified by the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Number of Neighbors</span> parameter. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"GET_SPATIAL_WEIGHTS_FROM_FILE\">Get spatial weights from file</span>—When <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Single dataset</span> is used as the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Input Tpe</span>, spatial relationships will be defined by a specified spatial weights matrix file. The path to the spatial weights file is specified by the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Weight Matrix File</span> parameter.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "neighborhood_type.domain.DISTANCE_BAND": "Distance band",
        "neighborhood_type.domain.GET_SPATIAL_WEIGHTS_FROM_FILE": "Get spatial weights from file",
        "neighborhood_type.domain.K_NEAREST_NEIGHBORS": "K nearest neighbors",
        "neighborhood_type.title": "Neighborhood Type",
        "number_of_neighbors.descr": "The number of neighbors around each feature that will be used to test for local relationships between categories. If no value is provided, the default of 8 is used. The provided value must be large enough to detect the relationships between features but small enough to still identify local patterns.",
        "number_of_neighbors.title": "Number of Neighbors",
        "number_of_permutations.descr": "<xdoc><p>The number of permutations that will be used to create a reference distribution. Choosing the number of permutations is a balance between precision and increased processing time. Choose your preference of speed versus precision. More robust and precise results take longer to calculate.</p><ul><li><span title=\"99\">99</span>—The analysis will use 99 permutations. With 99 permutations, the smallest possible pseudo p-value is 0.02 and all other pseudo p-values will be multiples of this value. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"199\">199</span>—The analysis will use 199 permutations. With 199 permutations, the smallest possible pseudo p-value is 0.01 and all other pseudo p-values will be even multiples of this value.</li><li><span title=\"499\">499</span>—The analysis will use 499 permutations. With 499 permutations, the smallest possible pseudo p-value is 0.004 and all other pseudo p-values will be even multiples of this value.</li><li><span title=\"999\">999</span>—The analysis will use 999 permutations. With 999 permutations, the smallest possible pseudo p-value is 0.002 and all other pseudo p-values will be even multiples of this value.</li><li><span title=\"9999\">9999</span>—The analysis will use 9,999 permutations. With 9,999 permutations, the smallest possible pseudo p-value is 0.0002 and all other pseudo p-values will be even multiples of this value.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "number_of_permutations.title": "Number of Permutations",
        "output_features.descr": "<xdoc><p>The output feature class containing all the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Input Features of Interest</span> with fields containing the resulting local colocation quotient, symbology bin, and p-values.</p></xdoc>",
        "output_features.title": "Output Features",
        "output_table.descr": "<xdoc><p>A table that includes the global colocation quotients between all the categories in the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Field of Interest</span> parameter and all the categories in the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Field Containing Neighboring Category</span> parameter. This table can help you determine the local categories to analyze.</p><p>If <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Datasets without categories</span> is used as the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Input Type</span> parameter value, global colocation quotients will be calculated for each dataset and between each dataset.</p></xdoc>",
        "output_table.title": "Output Table for Global Relationships",
        "params.category1": "Additional Options",
        "temporal_relationship_type.descr": "<xdoc><p>Specifies how temporal relationships among features will be defined.</p><ul><li><span title=\"BEFORE\">Before</span>—The time window will extend back in time for each of the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Input Features of Interest</span> values. Neighboring features must have a date/time stamp that occurs before the date/time stamp of the feature of interest to be included in the analysis. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"AFTER\">After</span>—The time window will extend forward in time for each of the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Input Features of Interest</span> values. Neighboring features must have a date/time stamp that occurs after the date/time stamp of the feature of interest to be included in the analysis.</li><li><span title=\"SPAN\">Span</span>—The time window will extend both back and forward in time for each of the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Input Features of Interest</span> values. Neighboring features that have a date/time stamp that occurs within the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Time Step Interval</span> value either before or after the date/time stamp of the feature of interest will be included in the analysis. For example, if the <span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Time Step Interval</span> parameter is set to 1 week, the window will look 1 week before and 1 week after the target feature.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "temporal_relationship_type.domain.AFTER": "After",
        "temporal_relationship_type.domain.BEFORE": "Before",
        "temporal_relationship_type.domain.SPAN": "Span",
        "temporal_relationship_type.title": "Temporal Relationship Type",
        "time_field_for_comparison.descr": "A date field with a time stamp for each feature to analyze your points using a space-time window. Features near each other in space and time will be considered neighbors and will be analyzed together.",
        "time_field_for_comparison.title": "Time Field of Neighboring Features",
        "time_field_of_interest.descr": "A date field with an optional time stamp for each feature to analyze points using a space-time window. Features near each other in space and time will be considered neighbors and will be analyzed together.",
        "time_field_of_interest.title": "Time Field of Interest",
        "time_step_interval.descr": "An integer and unit of measurement representing the number of time units composing the time window.",
        "time_step_interval.title": "Time Step Interval",
        "title": "Colocation Analysis",
        "weights_matrix_file.descr": "The path to a file containing weights that define spatial, and potentially temporal, relationships among features.",
        "weights_matrix_file.title": "Weight Matrix File"
    }
}
